{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 15, :comments ["<b>Seven days.</b> שטיינ\"א of days." "<b>Seven days you shall eat unleavened bread.</b> And in another place it says ״שֵׁשֶׁת יָמִים תֹּאכַל מַצּוֹת״ (Deuteronomy 16) — this teaches about the seventh day of Passover that there is no obligation to eat matzah, only that one may not eat leaven. From where do we know that even the six are optional? Scripture says ״שֵׁשֶׁת יָמִים״. This is a rule in Torah: a matter that was in the general rule and came out of the general rule to teach — it did not come out to teach about itself alone, but to teach about the entire general rule it came out. Just as the seventh is optional, so too the six are optional. One might think even the first night is optional — Scripture says ״בָּעֶרֶב תֹּאכְלוּ מַצֹּת״ — the verse fixed it as an obligation (Pesachim 120)." "<b>But on the first day you shall remove leaven.</b> From the eve of the festival, and it is called רִאשׁוֹן because it is before the seven; and we find that something earlier is called רִאשׁוֹן, as in ״הֲרִאישׁוֹן אָדָם תִּוָּלֵד״ (Job 15) — were you born before man? Or is it nothing but the first of the seven? Scripture says ״לֹא תִשְׁחַט עַל חָמֵץ״ (Exodus 34) — you shall not slaughter the paschal offering while leaven still exists (Pesachim 5)." "<b>That soul.</b> When it is of its own soul and awareness — excluding one who is coerced (Mekhilta)." "<b>From Israel.</b> I might understand that it is cut off from Israel and goes off to another nation — Scripture says in another place ״מִלְּפָנַי״ (Leviticus 22) — wherever My authority reaches."], :book :exodus, :voice :rashi, :lang :en, :chapter 12, :notes {0 "the la'az is Old French setaine, 'a week (of seven days)'"}, :source-witnesses nil, :timestamp "2026-08-02T08:51:41.940944625Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}